1
00:00:00,400 --> 00:00:02,860
Hawaiʻi ocean safety comes down to seven

2
00:00:02,710 --> 00:00:04,840
things to check before you swim.

3
00:00:05,760 --> 00:00:06,930
The gap in the waves.

4
00:00:07,390 --> 00:00:08,150
The waterline.

5
00:00:08,750 --> 00:00:09,460
The lifeguard.

6
00:00:09,950 --> 00:00:10,640
The season.

7
00:00:11,290 --> 00:00:12,360
The color of the water.

8
00:00:13,000 --> 00:00:13,600
The moon.

9
00:00:14,130 --> 00:00:14,850
And your buddy.

10
00:00:15,240 --> 00:00:16,680
Here is why each one matters.

11
00:00:16,870 --> 00:00:18,782
About two hundred visitors drowned in

12
00:00:18,632 --> 00:00:20,250
Hawaiʻi's ocean in five years,

13
00:00:20,580 --> 00:00:22,590
and the state tracked what each one was doing.

14
00:00:23,350 --> 00:00:23,870
Surfing.

15
00:00:24,210 --> 00:00:24,830
Scuba.

16
00:00:25,070 --> 00:00:25,630
Swimming.

17
00:00:26,060 --> 00:00:26,730
Snorkeling.

18
00:00:27,270 --> 00:00:28,490
Swept off the rocks.

19
00:00:28,860 --> 00:00:29,900
Jumping in from shore.

20
00:00:30,440 --> 00:00:32,125
One of these was the most common, and

21
00:00:31,975 --> 00:00:33,660
it is the one that looks the calmest.

22
00:00:34,140 --> 00:00:35,060
Hold your guess.

23
00:00:35,408 --> 00:00:36,748
What do you do in a rip current?

24
00:00:37,348 --> 00:00:39,098
Do not swim straight back to shore.

25
00:00:39,658 --> 00:00:40,888
A rip is a narrow

26
00:00:40,738 --> 00:00:41,968
current running out to sea,

27
00:00:42,198 --> 00:00:43,498
and it is faster than you.

28
00:00:44,108 --> 00:00:46,378
NOAA's advice is to swim along the shoreline

29
00:00:46,588 --> 00:00:47,968
until you are out of the pull,

30
00:00:48,278 --> 00:00:50,108
then angle back in with the waves.

31
00:00:50,428 --> 00:00:51,308
If you can't get out,

32
00:00:51,508 --> 00:00:51,958
float,

33
00:00:52,328 --> 00:00:53,268
face the shore,

34
00:00:53,398 --> 00:00:55,228
and wave or call for help.

35
00:00:55,808 --> 00:00:57,438
A rip does not pull you under.

36
00:00:57,818 --> 00:00:58,958
It pulls you out.

37
00:00:59,518 --> 00:01:01,158
You can often see one before you get in.

38
00:01:01,748 --> 00:01:02,618
Look at this beach.

39
00:01:02,728 --> 00:01:04,088
A, B or C:

40
00:01:04,658 --> 00:01:05,538
where would you get in?

41
00:01:06,478 --> 00:01:07,568
Many people pick B,

42
00:01:07,728 --> 00:01:09,808
the calm gap with no breaking waves.

43
00:01:10,378 --> 00:01:11,888
That gap is the rip.

44
00:01:12,538 --> 00:01:14,178
The waves are not breaking there because

45
00:01:14,028 --> 00:01:15,668
the water is deeper and running out.

46
00:01:16,288 --> 00:01:16,798
Check one:

47
00:01:17,218 --> 00:01:19,518
find the gap before you find your towel spot.

48
00:01:19,872 --> 00:01:21,202
What is shorebreak?

49
00:01:21,662 --> 00:01:23,545
It is when waves break directly on the

50
00:01:23,395 --> 00:01:25,062
shore instead of out in the water.

51
00:01:25,882 --> 00:01:29,552
Hawaiʻi's ocean safety office calls it unpredictable and dangerous,

52
00:01:29,762 --> 00:01:32,132
because the wave can drive you into the sand.

53
00:01:32,742 --> 00:01:34,102
The injuries are to the head,

54
00:01:34,212 --> 00:01:35,292
neck and spine,

55
00:01:35,642 --> 00:01:37,402
and they happen in knee deep water.

56
00:01:38,062 --> 00:01:40,052
Never dive into shorebreak head first.

57
00:01:40,552 --> 00:01:41,172
Instead,

58
00:01:41,442 --> 00:01:43,132
duck under the oncoming wave.

59
00:01:43,742 --> 00:01:45,432
If you get caught, stay calm,

60
00:01:45,672 --> 00:01:47,242
grab a breath between waves,

61
00:01:47,432 --> 00:01:49,562
and protect your head and neck with your arms.

62
00:01:50,562 --> 00:01:52,272
And the weather service adds one more:

63
00:01:52,692 --> 00:01:54,322
never turn your back on the ocean.

64
00:01:54,892 --> 00:01:55,522
Check two:

65
00:01:55,902 --> 00:01:57,585
watch the waterline for a full

66
00:01:57,435 --> 00:01:58,862
minute before you walk in.

67
00:01:59,216 --> 00:02:01,306
Which Hawaiʻi beaches have lifeguards?

68
00:02:01,836 --> 00:02:02,586
Not all of them.

69
00:02:03,206 --> 00:02:04,426
The state's first rule

70
00:02:04,526 --> 00:02:06,936
is to always swim at a lifeguarded beach,

71
00:02:07,246 --> 00:02:08,296
and it keeps a list

72
00:02:08,146 --> 00:02:09,196
of them for every island.

73
00:02:10,016 --> 00:02:11,206
The weather service calls

74
00:02:11,316 --> 00:02:12,136
lifeguards

75
00:02:12,276 --> 00:02:14,006
the best source of information

76
00:02:13,856 --> 00:02:15,586
about beach conditions and hazards.

77
00:02:16,246 --> 00:02:17,496
So check three is a question.

78
00:02:17,926 --> 00:02:18,826
Walk up and ask:

79
00:02:19,196 --> 00:02:20,286
where should I get in today?

80
00:02:21,267 --> 00:02:23,267
When are the waves biggest in Hawaiʻi?

81
00:02:23,877 --> 00:02:25,517
It depends which way the beach faces.

82
00:02:25,847 --> 00:02:27,577
Winter runs November through April,

83
00:02:27,687 --> 00:02:29,872
and it brings extremely high surf to

84
00:02:29,722 --> 00:02:31,617
many north and west shore beaches.

85
00:02:32,337 --> 00:02:34,107
Summer runs May through October,

86
00:02:34,417 --> 00:02:36,787
and the larger swells show up on south shores.

87
00:02:37,337 --> 00:02:39,817
So the calm beach from your friend's July photos

88
00:02:40,067 --> 00:02:41,517
can be closed out in January.

89
00:02:42,287 --> 00:02:44,637
Conditions may change quickly, even in one day.

90
00:02:45,087 --> 00:02:45,777
Check four:

91
00:02:46,047 --> 00:02:47,352
look at today's surf for

92
00:02:47,202 --> 00:02:48,507
that shore, not the photo.

93
00:02:48,856 --> 00:02:50,446
Can you swim after heavy rain?

94
00:02:51,316 --> 00:02:52,766
If the water is brown, no.

95
00:02:53,136 --> 00:02:54,726
Rain washes whatever is on

96
00:02:54,576 --> 00:02:56,166
the land into the ocean.

97
00:02:56,646 --> 00:02:59,814
The Department of Health says storm water can carry

98
00:02:59,664 --> 00:03:02,496
harmful germs from overflowing cesspools and animal waste.

99
00:03:02,856 --> 00:03:05,036
Its advice is to stay out of beaches or

100
00:03:05,116 --> 00:03:07,296
streams contaminated by brown water,

101
00:03:07,566 --> 00:03:09,056
and to watch for its advisories.

102
00:03:09,566 --> 00:03:11,450
A University of Hawaiʻi marine scientist

103
00:03:11,300 --> 00:03:12,896
puts it in six words.

104
00:03:13,316 --> 00:03:14,536
When the water is brown,

105
00:03:14,746 --> 00:03:15,596
turn around.

106
00:03:16,316 --> 00:03:16,956
Check five:

107
00:03:17,486 --> 00:03:18,847
look at the color of the

108
00:03:18,697 --> 00:03:19,856
water near any stream mouth.

109
00:03:20,207 --> 00:03:21,937
When do box jellyfish come in?

110
00:03:22,267 --> 00:03:23,047
On a schedule.

111
00:03:23,417 --> 00:03:25,717
About eight to ten days after each full moon,

112
00:03:25,927 --> 00:03:28,347
they swim to south and west shores to spawn.

113
00:03:29,017 --> 00:03:29,977
If one stings you,

114
00:03:30,156 --> 00:03:32,087
say there are three things within reach:

115
00:03:32,287 --> 00:03:32,967
vinegar,

116
00:03:33,087 --> 00:03:34,327
heat, or ice.

117
00:03:34,846 --> 00:03:35,677
Which do you grab?

118
00:03:36,367 --> 00:03:37,689
One of them made the damage

119
00:03:37,539 --> 00:03:38,667
more than twice as bad

120
00:03:38,857 --> 00:03:40,887
in a University of Hawaiʻi lab study.

121
00:03:41,397 --> 00:03:42,087
It was ice.

122
00:03:42,457 --> 00:03:44,101
Vinegar switches off the stinging cells

123
00:03:43,951 --> 00:03:45,347
that have not fired yet.

124
00:03:45,837 --> 00:03:47,877
Then pluck the tentacle off, do not rub,

125
00:03:48,017 --> 00:03:49,517
and use heat, not ice.

126
00:03:50,026 --> 00:03:51,607
Trouble breathing or chest pain,

127
00:03:51,776 --> 00:03:52,837
call 911.

128
00:03:53,526 --> 00:03:54,337
Check six:

129
00:03:54,656 --> 00:03:56,407
count the days since the full moon.

130
00:03:56,756 --> 00:03:58,056
Now the guess from the start.

131
00:03:58,266 --> 00:04:00,816
The most common activity in visitor ocean drownings

132
00:04:01,096 --> 00:04:01,906
was snorkeling.

133
00:04:02,526 --> 00:04:03,196
Not surfing.

134
00:04:03,366 --> 00:04:04,396
Not big waves.

135
00:04:05,106 --> 00:04:07,256
So is snorkeling a good idea for beginners?

136
00:04:08,016 --> 00:04:10,246
Calm water does not mean low risk.

137
00:04:10,726 --> 00:04:12,496
The Hawaiʻi Snorkel Safety Study,

138
00:04:12,756 --> 00:04:14,196
whose findings are preliminary,

139
00:04:14,476 --> 00:04:15,836
looked at how it happens.

140
00:04:16,406 --> 00:04:18,286
Snorkelers were found motionless,

141
00:04:18,565 --> 00:04:19,445
close to shore,

142
00:04:19,675 --> 00:04:20,925
in calm conditions,

143
00:04:21,125 --> 00:04:22,635
with no sign of distress.

144
00:04:23,546 --> 00:04:24,796
The study points to fluid

145
00:04:24,646 --> 00:04:25,895
building up in the lungs,

146
00:04:26,196 --> 00:04:27,995
which lowers oxygen fast.

147
00:04:28,716 --> 00:04:30,335
The warning signs are shortness of breath,

148
00:04:30,755 --> 00:04:31,466
fatigue,

149
00:04:31,726 --> 00:04:33,195
confusion and weakness.

150
00:04:33,875 --> 00:04:36,046
If you suddenly become short of breath or tired,

151
00:04:36,245 --> 00:04:37,596
signal for help and get

152
00:04:37,446 --> 00:04:38,796
out of the water immediately.

153
00:04:39,425 --> 00:04:40,976
Stay where you can touch the bottom.

154
00:04:41,346 --> 00:04:42,286
Swim with a buddy,

155
00:04:42,466 --> 00:04:43,726
and if your buddy goes still,

156
00:04:43,986 --> 00:04:44,655
check on them.

157
00:04:45,546 --> 00:04:46,236
Check seven:

158
00:04:46,526 --> 00:04:47,536
before you snorkel,

159
00:04:47,776 --> 00:04:48,455
pick a buddy

160
00:04:48,546 --> 00:04:49,675
and agree on a signal.

161
00:04:50,023 --> 00:04:51,223
So how do you check all of

162
00:04:51,073 --> 00:04:52,123
that before you leave your room?

163
00:04:52,703 --> 00:04:54,493
That is what we built OceanSafe for.

164
00:04:54,933 --> 00:04:56,983
It is free, there is no sign in,

165
00:04:57,203 --> 00:04:59,223
and it covers Kauaʻi, Oʻahu,

166
00:04:59,353 --> 00:04:59,973
Maui

167
00:05:00,123 --> 00:05:01,233
and Hawaiʻi Island.

168
00:05:01,883 --> 00:05:02,653
Pick a beach

169
00:05:02,903 --> 00:05:04,193
and it shows today's surf,

170
00:05:04,403 --> 00:05:05,013
wind,

171
00:05:05,163 --> 00:05:07,063
tide and rain for that shore,

172
00:05:07,413 --> 00:05:08,713
whether there is a lifeguard,

173
00:05:08,983 --> 00:05:10,353
and the hazards we just covered:

174
00:05:10,923 --> 00:05:12,683
known rip channels, brown water,

175
00:05:12,973 --> 00:05:14,153
and jellyfish days.

176
00:05:14,853 --> 00:05:16,703
It will never tell you a beach is safe.

177
00:05:17,163 --> 00:05:19,113
It shows what the conditions data says,

178
00:05:19,473 --> 00:05:20,577
and then it tells you the

179
00:05:20,427 --> 00:05:21,373
same thing the state does.

180
00:05:21,843 --> 00:05:22,993
Ask the lifeguard.

181
00:05:23,829 --> 00:05:24,489
Seven

182
00:05:24,599 --> 00:05:25,249
checks.

183
00:05:26,579 --> 00:05:27,839
The gap in the waves.

184
00:05:28,199 --> 00:05:28,989
The waterline.

185
00:05:29,559 --> 00:05:30,299
The lifeguard.

186
00:05:30,759 --> 00:05:31,509
The season.

187
00:05:31,919 --> 00:05:32,759
The water color.

188
00:05:33,149 --> 00:05:33,849
The moon.

189
00:05:34,579 --> 00:05:35,309
And your buddy.

190
00:05:36,229 --> 00:05:36,949
Next video:

191
00:05:37,319 --> 00:05:38,942
why the calmest looking activity is

192
00:05:38,792 --> 00:05:40,169
the one in the data,

193
00:05:40,509 --> 00:05:42,029
and how to snorkel with that in mind.

194
00:05:42,689 --> 00:05:44,009
Which beach are you headed to?

195
00:05:44,359 --> 00:05:45,529
Tell us in the comments.

